Think about the people you interact with, a colleague, a neighbor, a stranger on a train.

39:10.680 --> 39:12.680
That's a fascinating way to look at it.

39:12.800 --> 39:12.940
Right.

39:13.360 --> 39:21.360
How quickly do you rely on passive observation, the clothes they wear, a passing comment they make, their job title, to compress them into a neat molar category?

39:22.000 --> 39:29.960
You territorialize their infinite molecular complexity into a rigid stereotype because, frankly, it reduces your cognitive load.

39:30.180 --> 39:31.620
It saves your internal bandwidth.

39:31.840 --> 39:32.560
We all do it.

39:32.780 --> 39:36.200
But in doing so, you are subjecting them to admissibility distortion.

39:36.200 --> 39:38.960
You assume you know their reachability set.

39:39.380 --> 39:47.700
You assume you know exactly how they will react to a crisis or what they are capable of achieving based entirely on a passive observational data set.

39:47.800 --> 39:48.760
And you really don't.

39:48.880 --> 39:49.260
You don't.

39:49.460 --> 39:53.700
You have no idea what their actual intensive geometries are until you intervene.

39:53.700 --> 40:06.940
Until you step out of your passive observation, shatter the steady state dynamic and actually engage them, ask a disruptive question, offer an unexpected vulnerability, introduce an intervention into the system.

40:07.320 --> 40:10.240
The people around you are not simply the sum of what you see.

40:10.660 --> 40:13.420
